    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional owner email support so that the app satisfies the spec: an owner *may* include `email`; when present it must be syntactically valid, stored/returned lower-cased, and a present-but-invalid value is rejected with 400.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an `email` property to the `OwnerFields` schema (`type: string, format: email, maxLength: 255`, not required). Because `Owner` composes `OwnerFields` via `allOf`, this gives both the create-input DTO (`OwnerFieldsDto`) and the response DTO (`OwnerDto`) an `email` field. The `format: email` also makes the generator emit `@Email` bean validation.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added an `email` field (`@Column(name = \"email\")`) with getter/setter and included it in `toString()`. MapStruct's `OwnerMapper` maps it automatically by name (both directions), so no mapper change was needed.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added a `normalizeEmail` helper that leaves a `null` email untouched (optional), lower-cases a valid one (`Locale.ROOT`), and throws `InvalidFieldsException` (\u2192 400 via the existing advice) when present but invalid. It's applied in both `addOwner` and `updateOwner`, mirroring the existing telephone-normalization pattern.\n\n4. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `email` column to the `owners` table.\n\n5. **Seed data** (`hsqldb`, `mysql` `data.sql`) \u2014 converted the positional `INSERT INTO owners VALUES (...)` statements to explicit column lists so they keep working after the new column was added (this was the initial context-load failure).\n\nTest results: all 3 acceptance tests (`coreLowercasesEmail`, `errorRejectsInvalidEmail`, `functionalityAcceptsMissingEmail`) pass, and the full `./mvnw test` build is **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNote: with `@Email` now on the generated DTO, an invalid email is actually rejected at the Bean Validation layer (still a 400), and the controller's own check is a belt-and-suspenders guarantee plus the source of the lower-casing.",
